    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a burrito\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a rotisserie shortcut that tastes like you worked harder than you did. The bird does the heavy lifting, the crema costs pennies, and the tortilla keeps the carbs low.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "Rotisserie birds are the cheat code here. Two birds pull enough skinless meat for the batch and usually cost less than the same weight of raw breast once you factor in that they are already cooked. Pull the skin off before you weigh so the numbers stay honest.",
                       "Cottage cheese is the quiet hero of this crema. Buy the tub, not the individual cups, and blend it smooth so nobody at the table knows it is not sour cream.",
                       "Salsa verde in the jar is cheaper and more consistent than roasting your own tomatillos, and this batch uses close to a full jar so nothing goes fuzzy in the fridge.",
                       "Bell peppers cost less in the three color bag than loose singles, and you only need the red one here, so the rest carries into next week\u0027s cooking."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Pull all the meat off the rotisserie chicken and discard the skin and bones. Weigh out the skinless meat and shred it with two forks.",
                    "Warm the olive oil in a large skillet over medium heat and cook the diced onion and red bell pepper for 6 to 8 minutes until soft.",
                    "Add the minced garlic, ground cumin and garlic powder and stir for one minute, then stir in the thawed spinach.",
                    "Add the shredded chicken and the salsa verde, season with the salt, and warm it all through. Pull off the heat and stir in the Mexican cheese blend so it melts into the filling.",
                    "Blend the cottage cheese, Greek yogurt, lime juice and chopped cilantro until smooth to make the crema.",
                    "Set the filling bowl on the scale, subtract the empty weight, and divide the number by 14. Lay a stripe of that much filling down each tortilla, spoon on a share of the crema, fold in the sides and roll tight.",
                    "Wrap each burrito in foil or parchment and portion into containers."
                ],
    "portion_html":  "The honest way to keep 14 burritos equal is to weigh the filling, not eyeball the scoop. Set the bowl on the scale, subtract the empty weight, divide by 14, and give each tortilla that much before you add the crema and roll. Each burrito carries \u003cstrong\u003e{{cal}} calories and {{protein}} grams of protein\u003c/strong\u003e. Reheat wrapped for a minute, then open and crisp the seam in a dry pan if you like.",
